package mop;

import java.security.KeyPair;
import java.security.PrivateKey;
import java.security.PublicKey;

import br.unb.cic.mop.eh.*;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateStore;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateVerdict;
import br.unb.cic.mop.Property;

/**
 * KeyPairSpec
 *
 * Monitors java.security.KeyPair against the CrySL rule KeyPair.crysl.
 *
 * ORDER. The rule orders `Con, (GetPubl | GetPriv)*`: the two-argument constructor, then any
 * number of `getPublic()` and `getPrivate()` calls. The constructor is mandatory here, as the
 * rule writes it. A sequence the automaton does not accept is reported under
 * `KEYPAIR-ORDER-01` when no construction of the pair was observed and under
 * `KEYPAIR-ORDER-00` otherwise; both codes are registered in `codes.csv`.
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS. The rule states none, so this specification checks no value.
 *
 * REQUIRES `generatedPrivkey[privateKey];` and `generatedPubkey[publicKey];`, both read at the
 * constructor over the two halves a program hands to `new KeyPair(...)`. They are separate
 * claims about separate objects and carry a code each.
 *
 * ENSURES three clauses, two of which have a site here.
 * `generatedPubkey[retPublicKey] after GetPubl;` is written at `gpu` over the key
 * `getPublic()` returned and `generatedPrivkey[retPrivateKey] after GetPriv;` at `gpr` over the
 * key `getPrivate()` returned: the key was produced by a call the instrumentation observed.
 * `generatedKeypair[this, _] after Con;` has no site: no rule this set transcribes requires
 * that predicate, so a write here would record a fact nothing reads.
 *
 * Reach limits and divergences from the rule.
 * - A program almost never constructs the `KeyPair` it reads from; it takes one out of
 *   `KeyPairGenerator.generateKeyPair()`, a call no event of this specification observes. Such
 *   a pair reaches `getPublic()`/`getPrivate()` from the start state, so the call is reported
 *   as a wrong call sequence under `KEYPAIR-ORDER-01`. Following the rule's ORDER as written
 *   is what makes that report common.
 * - The two ENSURES writes sit in the event bodies, which run before the transition is
 *   decided, so both halves are marked even on a pair whose sequence the automaton rejects:
 *   the ordering report costs the chain nothing.
 * - Either read at the constructor answers NOT_OBSERVED when the half came from a producer the
 *   weaving does not reach. That is a reach limit and not a misuse, which is why it carries a
 *   code of its own.
 *
 * @see https://github.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/blob/6d844ab402229aaefa4c5e45bf080987b787624b/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/KeyPair.crysl
 */
KeyPairSpec(KeyPair keyPair) {

    // Set by the one creation event, `c1`, and never cleared: the generated `reset()` restores
    // the automaton state and leaves this field, so the fact survives a failure. `@fail` reads
    // it to report a pair whose construction no event of this specification observed -- a pair
    // out of `KeyPairGenerator.generateKeyPair()` is one -- under `KEYPAIR-ORDER-01`
    // (`creation-unobserved`) instead of the wrong-sequence code, on the first failure and on
    // every later one.
    boolean creationObserved = false;

    // The rule's `c1: KeyPair(publicKey, privateKey);`, its one `Con` event, and the site of
    // both REQUIRES clauses: `generatedPrivkey[privateKey];` and `generatedPubkey[publicKey];`.
    // They are the constructor's clauses -- what a program may hand to `new KeyPair(...)` --
    // and they are separate claims about separate objects, so they carry a code each rather
    // than being folded into one composite report.
    //
    // Each read asks what its clause asks: whether the key was produced by a call the
    // instrumentation observed. A private key is marked by `KeyFactorySpec` at `genPrivate`,
    // by `KeyStoreSpec` when the key it retrieves is a `PrivateKey`, and by `gpr` below; a
    // public key by `KeyFactorySpec` at `genPublic` and by `gpu` below. Both clauses are
    // one-place in every rule that ensures them, so there is no value place: the bound key is
    // compared by identity, and only the very object a producing call handed out carries the
    // mark.
    //
    // The reads are in the event body and not in a `condition(...)` guard: a guard compiles to
    // an early return ahead of both the body and the transition, so a key pair whose halves
    // the monitor never saw would drop the constructor out of the automaton and the
    // `getPublic()` that follows would be reported as a wrong call sequence instead of as the
    // unsatisfied requirement it is. A failed REQUIRES is not a typestate failure.
    //
    // The composition the clause describes survives at run time, which is what makes the read
    // meaningful: `new KeyPair(kp.getPublic(), kp.getPrivate())` over a generated pair runs,
    // `getPublic()` and `getPrivate()` return the same object on every call, and the
    // constructor stores the halves without copying, so what `gpu`/`gpr` marked is the very
    // object this read asks about.
    //
    // Each verdict is three-valued and its two accusing outcomes are different claims.
    // VIOLATED is positive evidence about this key -- an entry exists and what it records is
    // not what the clause demands -- reported under `KEYPAIR-CONSTR-00` for the private half
    // and `KEYPAIR-CONSTR-01` for the public one. NOT_OBSERVED says only that nothing about
    // the key was ever recorded, which happens when its producing call was not woven or runs
    // inside a library the weaving does not reach -- reported under `KEYPAIR-NOBS-00` and
    // `KEYPAIR-NOBS-01`. All four codes are registered in `codes.csv`.
    //
    // `returning(KeyPair keyPair)` and not `returning(KeyPair kp)`: the generator keys a
    // parametric monitor on the event parameters whose NAMES are the specification's declared
    // parameters, so a `KeyPair` bound under any other name binds no monitored object at all.
    // The event would then get the parameterless map and run on the root monitor and on every
    // live monitor of the specification, instead of reaching the pair it constructs.
    event c1 after(PublicKey publicKey, PrivateKey privateKey) returning(KeyPair keyPair):
      call(public KeyPair.new(PublicKey, PrivateKey))
      && args(publicKey, privateKey) {
        creationObserved = true;
        PredicateVerdict privOrigin = PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.GENERATED_PRIVATE_KEY, privateKey);
        if (privOrigin == PredicateVerdict.VIOLATED) {
            ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "KeyPairSpec", "" + __LOC,
                "v=1 code=KEYPAIR-CONSTR-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=KeyPair val='' exp='a private key produced by one of the generators the rule names' msg='the private key given to the KeyPair constructor carries a key-origin predicate the rule does not admit'"));
        }
        else if (privOrigin == PredicateVerdict.NOT_OBSERVED) {
            ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "KeyPairSpec", "" + __LOC,
                "v=1 code=KEYPAIR-NOBS-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=KeyPair val='' exp='a private key produced by one of the generators the rule names' msg='no generator of the private key given to the KeyPair constructor was observed'" + Evidence.keysFor(privateKey)));
        }
        PredicateVerdict pubOrigin = PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.GENERATED_PUBLIC_KEY, publicKey);
        if (pubOrigin == PredicateVerdict.VIOLATED) {
            ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "KeyPairSpec", "" + __LOC,
                "v=1 code=KEYPAIR-CONSTR-01 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=KeyPair val='' exp='a public key produced by one of the generators the rule names' msg='the public key given to the KeyPair constructor carries a key-origin predicate the rule does not admit'"));
        }
        else if (pubOrigin == PredicateVerdict.NOT_OBSERVED) {
            ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "KeyPairSpec", "" + __LOC,
                "v=1 code=KEYPAIR-NOBS-01 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=KeyPair val='' exp='a public key produced by one of the generators the rule names' msg='no generator of the public key given to the KeyPair constructor was observed'" + Evidence.keysFor(publicKey)));
        }
    }

    // `ENSURES generatedPubkey[retPublicKey] after GetPubl;` over the key `getPublic()`
    // returned. What the write establishes, for the readers in `CipherSpec`,
    // `KeyAgreementSpec`, `SignatureSpec`, `TrustAnchorSpec`, `KeySpec` and `c1` above: the
    // public key was produced by a call the instrumentation observed. The bound place is the
    // key itself, compared by identity; the clause has no value place.
    //
    // The write is in the event body and not at the acceptance point, and that placement is
    // what keeps the chain alive. A program almost never constructs the `KeyPair` it reads
    // from: it takes one out of `KeyPairGenerator.generateKeyPair()`, a call this
    // specification does not observe, so `gpu` fires from the start state and the automaton
    // rejects the sequence. An event body runs before the transition is decided, so the write
    // happens anyway; a write in a handler would not, and `generatedPubkey` would go unwritten
    // for every generated pair. The ordering report and the predicate are independent of each
    // other: a pair that draws `KEYPAIR-ORDER-01` here still marks both of its halves.
    event gpu after(KeyPair keyPair) returning(PublicKey publicKey):
      call(public PublicKey KeyPair.getPublic()) &&
      target(keyPair) {
        PredicateStore.instance().ensure(Property.GENERATED_PUBLIC_KEY, publicKey);
    }

    // `ENSURES generatedPrivkey[retPrivateKey] after GetPriv;` over the key `getPrivate()`
    // returned. What the write establishes, for the readers in `CipherSpec`,
    // `KeyAgreementSpec`, `SignatureSpec`, `KeySpec` and `c1` above: the private key was
    // produced by a call the instrumentation observed. The bound place is the key itself,
    // compared by identity; the clause has no value place.
    //
    // The predicate is the private key's own and not the public key's. They are separate
    // clauses of the rule and separate readers depend on them: `SignatureSpec` asks for
    // `generatedPrivkey` at `initSign` and for `generatedPubkey` at `initVerify`, so a private
    // key marked as public would answer NOT_OBSERVED to the first and SATISFIED to the second.
    //
    // Same body placement as `gpu`, for the same reason: a pair a program obtained from
    // `KeyPairGenerator.generateKeyPair()` reaches this event from the start state, and an
    // event body runs before the transition is decided, so the mark is written even though the
    // sequence is rejected.
    event gpr after(KeyPair keyPair) returning(PrivateKey privateKey):
      call(public PrivateKey KeyPair.getPrivate()) &&
      target(keyPair) {
        PredicateStore.instance().ensure(Property.GENERATED_PRIVATE_KEY, privateKey);
    }

    // The rule's ORDER, `Con, (GetPubl | GetPriv)*`. The constructor is mandatory, which is
    // what the rule orders: a pair reaches `getPublic()`/`getPrivate()` legally only after the
    // `c1` that built it.
    //
    // What that costs is stated once, here. Practically every Android key pair comes out of
    // `KeyPairGenerator.generateKeyPair()`, which fires no `c1` at all, so every
    // `getPublic()`/`getPrivate()` on such a pair is a sequence this automaton rejects, and
    // the report is common over real applications. No `c1` reaches that monitor, so it carries
    // `KEYPAIR-ORDER-01` (`creation-unobserved`), which separates it from a constructed pair
    // used out of order.
    //
    // What it does not cost is the predicate chain: the event bodies of `gpu` and `gpr` run
    // before the transition is decided, so both halves of a rejected pair are still marked and
    // every downstream read of `generatedPubkey`/`generatedPrivkey` answers as it would for an
    // accepted one.
    //
    // JavaMOP offers `creation event c1`, which would instantiate a monitor only at the
    // constructor and so report no ordering failure at all. It is not used: without a monitor
    // the event body does not run, and this specification is the only producer of
    // `generatedPubkey` and `generatedPrivkey` over the halves of a `KeyPair`, so the reads in
    // `SignatureSpec`, `CipherSpec` and `KeyAgreementSpec` would fall to NOT_OBSERVED. That
    // trades a report the rule's own ORDER states for the loss of a live chain.
    ere: c1 (gpu | gpr)*

    // A call sequence the automaton rejects. The two codes separate the cases a reader has to
    // tell apart: no construction of this pair was observed at all (`KEYPAIR-ORDER-01`, what a
    // pair out of `KeyPairGenerator.generateKeyPair()` draws) and a constructed pair used out
    // of order (`KEYPAIR-ORDER-00`). `creationObserved` is not touched by the generated
    // `reset()`, so the distinction holds on every later failure as well. Both codes are
    // registered in `codes.csv`.
    @fail {
       if (!creationObserved) {
           ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.InvalidSequenceOfMethodCalls, "KeyPairSpec", "" + __LOC,
               "v=1 code=KEYPAIR-ORDER-01 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=KeyPair val='' exp='' msg='the observed call sequence is not one KeyPairSpec accepts and no construction of the key pair was observed'"));
       }
       else {
           ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.InvalidSequenceOfMethodCalls, "KeyPairSpec", "" + __LOC,
               "v=1 code=KEYPAIR-ORDER-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=KeyPair val='' exp='' msg='the observed call sequence is not one KeyPairSpec accepts'"));
       }
       __RESET;
    }

    // The acceptance point of this specification, declared and empty. No clause of the rule is
    // established here: `generatedPubkey[retPublicKey] after GetPubl;` and
    // `generatedPrivkey[retPrivateKey] after GetPriv;` are written in the bodies of `gpu` and
    // `gpr`, and `generatedKeypair[this, _] after Con;` has no site at all, because no rule
    // this set transcribes requires that predicate. The handler is kept because an `ere` names
    // its accepting category whether or not a handler uses it.
    @match {
    }


}